Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Figure 1.2.  The BPE merge tree for the token 'the': \
three pairwise merges combine four characters (t, h, e, </w>) into one merged \
token, then the same three rules are reapplied to every occurrence of 'the' \
in the corpus.",
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic]], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"c245d48e-9d39-43a6-afff-\
55892e527f19"],

Cell["\<\
Three Wolfram Language idioms make the implementation concise:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bc67e474-a10b-4546-8c24-0724802eebbc"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\[FilledCircle] ",
 StyleBox["Counts[preTokenise[text]]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " builds the pre-token frequency map in one expression.\n",
 "\[FilledCircle] ",
 StyleBox["Merge[..., Total]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " sums values over duplicate keys; the keys can be arbitrary ",
 "expressions including a two-element list ",
 StyleBox["{\"t\", \"h\"}",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ".\n",
 "\[FilledCircle] ",
 StyleBox["SequenceReplace[syms, {a, b} -> a <> b]",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " merges a pair inside a symbol list.  No two-pointer loop, no off-by-one."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"35b2167e-c379-4f5c-8fa3-36feb2d2b3b1"],

Cell["\<\
Train BPE on the corpus.  4000 merges takes ~12 seconds on a Mac mini thanks \
to incremental pair-count maintenance.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0c7289da-e67e-4961-a4b5-2ee2c1bc3d58"],

Cell["\<\
bpe = Import[\"part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/data/bpe_trained.mx\"];
mergeRules = bpe[\"MergeRules\"];
vocab = LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`buildBPEVocabulary[bpe];
tokId = LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`tokenToIdAssociation[vocab];
{Length[mergeRules], Length[vocab]}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"8b37f3f2-ce4c-47bb-b461-fb8c554870c7"],

Cell["\<\
4000 merge rules, 4064 final vocabulary entries (64 base characters + 4000 \
merged tokens).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"573ccd63-d974-4793-9086-69a313e60628"],

Cell["\<\
The first few merge rules are the highest-frequency bigrams of Shakespeare:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d1790d80-9bb3-4e01-bf3b-a74b67b5aec8"],

Cell["Take[mergeRules, 8]", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"f2407e03-80c0-4367-a0ce-aabf303aaeea"],

Cell["\<\
Words ending in -e, the bigram th, ' ' followed by certain words, etc.  In \
any English corpus the first hundred BPE merges all look like this.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"0d482645-874e-4574-aeb0-a35609049d0e"],

Cell["1.3  See the tokens", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"c798fab7-4a15-426d-af62-53aa2355551e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The clearest way to convey what BPE has done is to look at the ",
 "actual output.  Each coloured box is one BPE token; the small ",
 "black square at the end of a box marks the end-of-word boundary ",
 "(the ",
 StyleBox["</w>",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 " marker).  Identical tokens get identical colours."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"165796aa-6cd9-48a7-b883-ea64fb678878"],

Cell["\<\
LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`colouredTokens[
  \"To be or not to be, that is the question.\", mergeRules]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a6033e3c-dd6e-484a-95f9-8d83987018d6"],

Cell["\<\
Drag the slider to retokenise the same passage at different merge counts.  At \
0 merges every box is a single character; at the maximum, common short words \
collapse to a single token each.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"25293ab5-2ac1-4a96-8b67-aeea897c8f20"],

Cell["\<\
Manipulate[
  LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`colouredTokens[
    \"To be or not to be, that is the question.\",
    Take[mergeRules, n]],
  {{n, 500, \"merge rules used\"}, 0, Length[mergeRules], 50,
   Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
  ControlPlacement -> Top]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"a147ed52-bd74-4f1c-b850-5d701d4d3085"],

Cell["\<\
Watch BPE assemble \"speaking\" out of characters one merge at a time:\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"06b47dc4-875c-44ff-941b-4797b5f63ca5"],

Cell["\<\
With[{prog = LLMAtoZ`Tokeniser`mergeProgression[
    \"speaking\", Take[mergeRules, 500]]},
  Animate[
    Framed[
      Style[Row[Riffle[prog[[step]], \"  \"]], 18, FontFamily -> \
\"Courier\"],
      FrameMargins -> 12],
    {{step, 1, \"merges applied\"}, 1, Length[prog], 1,
     Appearance -> \"Labeled\"},
    AnimationRate -> 1.5, ControlPlacement -> Top]
]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b0b870a8-4f85-4a1c-a27f-a90f1e5cd5d0"],

Cell["1.4  The vocab-sequence trade-off", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"cda712e6-e084-459a-8cf5-8d872c81b82e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Train BPE with progressively more merges and the sequence length ",
 "drops as the vocabulary grows.  The shape of the curve is the ",
 "empirical justification for sub-word tokenisation.  The data ",
 "below was computed once and cached in ",
 StyleBox["part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/data/vocab_seqlen_sweep.json",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 "; we add the character and word-level extremes from the loaded ",
 "corpus and plot them on a log\[Dash]log scale."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"40854717-03da-476d-bb5d-e4873d51ab98"],

Cell["\<\
sweep = Import[
  \"part-01-tokeniser-and-embeddings/data/vocab_seqlen_sweep.json\",
  \"RawJSON\"];
bpePoints  = {#[\"vocab\"], #[\"seqLen\"]} & /@ sweep;
charPoint  = {65, StringLength[text]};
wordPoint  = {Length[Union[StringSplit[text]]], Length[StringSplit[text]]};
ListLogLogPlot[
  {Prepend[bpePoints, charPoint], {wordPoint}},
  Frame -> True, GridLines -> Automatic,
  FrameLabel -> {\"vocabulary size |V|\",
                  \"sequence length n (tokens)\"},
  PlotLabel -> \"Tiny Shakespeare: vocab\\[Dash]seqlen trade-off\",
  PlotStyle -> {
    Directive[ColorData[97][1], PointSize[0.018]],
    Directive[ColorData[97][2], PointSize[0.025]]},
  Joined -> {True, False},
  PlotLegends -> Placed[
    {\"char  +  BPE sweep (0\\[Dash]4000 merges)\", \"word-level\"},
    {Right, Bottom}],
  ImageSize -> 850]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"b9427066-c15d-4462-af85-987b4dde7fd3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Figure 1.3.",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 "  Character (top-left corner: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"b2ad7963-18b2-4637-879b-9ca26916798e"],
 " = 65, ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"a1165ee5-a5df-4f6d-806a-ac82da394785"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 1.16 M), word (bottom-right corner: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"|", "V", "|"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"b6cbc7f0-9d0b-462a-9c26-c38b7a6ca3cd"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 14.5 k, ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"f8245866-ba48-4b11-87da-ebaed3e70cb3"],
 " \[TildeEqual] 252 k), ",
 "and BPE (the curve between, one point per merge budget).  ",
 "The interior of the curve is where every modern tokeniser lives."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bb5d6142-3858-4c31-9439-413282d50636"],

Cell["\<\
Numerical picture: at 50 merges the sequence is already 769k tokens (a third \
shorter than character) for the price of 50 extra vocabulary entries.  At \
4000 merges, 299k tokens, six times shorter than character.  The curve \
flattens because later merges chase increasingly rare bigrams.  Practical \
models pick an interior point: GPT-2 uses 50,257 merges, GPT-4 uses 100,256.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"74632851-5503-4c5f-9128-453ebe89bdbb"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The information-theoretic shape of this curve is not accidental.  ",
 "Tiny Shakespeare is approximately Zipfian (the ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"dd85520c-df3b-493e-b6d3-75ce12f37b91"],
 "-th most frequent word has about 1/",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"1954e20e-e9b1-4601-8c97-4d6baf080c1b"],
 " the frequency of the most frequent), so the marginal compression ",
 "from the ",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"5811f175-fa56-411f-aa4b-f4790ca68bef"],
 "-th merge falls as 1/",
 Cell[BoxData["n"], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"1f7eb4e2-0054-4254-a20a-c662eeff5df7"],
 ".  Total compression grows as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"log", "(", "n", ")"}]], "Text",
  FontSize->14,ExpressionUUID->"c9cd530e-b8d0-449c-a7f6-8e99fbb7f5b5"],
 " \[LongDash] the gentle downward drift toward the word-level ",
 "corner."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"fe185706-ea42-4435-9574-2236ab9afc54"],

Cell["1.5  Character-level as the small-model alternative", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"d20a2fa0-129a-4ad0-8ad2-25a0ce6fe60b"],

Cell["\<\
BPE is the right choice for any model with a parameter budget in the tens or \
hundreds of millions.  For a small model (say 1\[Dash]5 M parameters) trained \
on a Shakespeare-sized corpus, the embedding tables \[LongDash] one row per \
vocabulary entry \[LongDash] consume most of the parameter budget, leaving \
very little for the transformer to actually compute with.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"ed31552c-1203-4991-a425-d815b735d662"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A character-level vocabulary shrinks the embedding tables by a ",
 "factor of ~60.  The trade-off is much longer sequences, but at ",
 "this scale the per-parameter modelling capacity is what matters.  ",
 "This is the choice Karpathy made in ",
 StyleBox["char-rnn",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " (2015) at LSTM ~1M parameters on the same corpus, and the choice ",
 "we will make for our from-scratch trained model later in this ",
 "notebook."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"91de1466-6581-4702-8e79-01db679d7eb5"],

Cell["\<\
charVocab = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charVocabulary[text];
charTokId = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charTokenToIdAssociation[charVocab];
charIdTok = LLMAtoZ`CharTokeniser`charIdToTokenAssociation[charVocab];
{Length[charVocab], First[charVocab[[1]]] // Quiet}\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"44f05374-a0f9-442b-af15-7589a68a4725"],

Cell["2.  Embeddings: integers to vectors", "Chapter",ExpressionUUID->"b9a1ee66-b5d8-4a2d-8b0c-b627b7c3b124"],

Cell["\<\
A transformer does not see token IDs; it sees vectors.  Once we have a list \
of integer token IDs, we feed them through two embedding tables in parallel: \
a token embedding (one row per vocabulary entry, learnable) and a positional \
embedding (one row per position in the sequence, learnable).  The model input \
is the elementwise sum.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"81c234ae-1017-4089-8c33-f71971a36af1"],

